# OpenBao policy for the slack-admin AppRole — read + update + create on
# exactly one secrets-external KV-v2 entry (platform/slack-admin), the Slack
# app configuration token pair (app_config_token, app_config_refresh_token,
# rotated_at). Read-only would not suffice: the access token expires after
# 12 hours, and rotating it consumes the refresh token (single-use) and
# returns a brand-new pair that must be written back — without update, the
# credential would work for 12 hours and then fail permanently with no way
# to recover. Create is needed too: KV-v2 has no path until something writes
# version 1 of it, and that first write requires the create capability —
# update alone can only overwrite a version that already exists, so without
# create this identity could never seed its own entry. No delete, no list,
# and no wildcard: this identity can create/rotate the one secret it owns
# and cannot see or touch any other path on either mount.

path "secrets-external/data/platform/slack-admin" {
  capabilities = ["read", "update", "create"]
}

# The rotation-clock stamp: after each successful rotation the generic
# rotator PATCHes custom_metadata.rotated_<field> for BOTH fields it just
# wrote (rotated_app_config_token, rotated_app_config_refresh_token — the
# pair rotates together in one CAS write, so both get their own clock) via
# the KV-v2 metadata PATCH primitive, which needs only `patch`. `read` lets
# the age auditor (secret-age-audit) and any future on-box freshness check
# read those same stamps back. allowed_parameters constrains the PATCH body
# to custom_metadata only: the metadata PATCH endpoint also accepts
# max_versions/cas_required/delete_version_after, and this identity's own
# `update` on data/ above means an unconstrained `patch` here would let it
# set max_versions=1 and then destroy every prior version with one CAS
# write — no `delete` capability required.
path "secrets-external/metadata/platform/slack-admin" {
  capabilities       = ["read", "patch"]
  allowed_parameters = { "custom_metadata" = [] }
}

# create + read on platform/slack-app-* only — the per-app credential set
# (client_id, client_secret, verification_token, signing_secret) issued once
# by apps.manifest.create for a given app_id, one entry per app. Create only,
# no update: these values are never rotated, so a second write to the same
# app_id would only ever mean an accidental overwrite of a credential set
# that cannot be recovered any other way. No delete, no list, and scoped to
# this one path prefix — not the whole mount.
path "secrets-external/data/platform/slack-app-*" {
  capabilities = ["create", "read"]
}
